You are helping read what a document claims. You are given its
sentences, each numbered (S1, S2, ...). They keep the writer's own
marking as Markdown: *italic* (emphasis, a title, a term or a foreign
word), **bold**, and ^[...] for a footnote, an aside to the sentence it
is in; □ stands for a citation or a cross-reference. Read them as the
writer meant them: a stressed *not* or *only* is part of what the
sentence says. Write down every claim the text asserts, and every claim
it gives as someone else's, in propositional logic, as a file a prover
will read. You do not decide whether the claims are true or whether
they follow from each other. Your task is only to write down faithfully
what the text says, all of it: not one argument, but each thing it
holds or reports.

The file has one line per statement, fields separated by a TAB:

atom<TAB>name<TAB>what it stands for
P1<TAB>S3<TAB>formula
P2<TAB>S5<TAB>formula

- An atom is one plain claim that is true or false, named in lower
  case letters, digits and _ (patched, safe_to_deploy). Say what it
  stands for in the text's own words. The same claim in other words
  is the same atom; a different claim is a different atom.
- A word used in two senses makes two claims. Where the text uses a
  word in one sense in one place and in another sense elsewhere (a
  bank as a river's edge and as a lender; free as costing nothing and
  as unconstrained), give each sense its own atom and say in a comment
  which word changes its sense. Never join two senses to make the
  argument work.
- Each claim the text states is a premise (P1, P2, ...), with the
  number of the sentence that states it, whether the text argues for
  it, from it, or neither: a definition, a thesis, a report of fact,
  a conclusion it draws. A sentence that states two claims gives two
  premises. Write no conclusion line (C): this is a list of what the
  text holds, not an argument.
- Write no assumption (A) for what the text does not state.
- Formulas use ! (not), & (and), | (or), -> (if ... then),
  <-> (if and only if) and parentheses. "If p, q", "q if p" and
  "p only if q" are all p -> q: "if it rains, the street is wet" is
  rain -> wet, and so is "it rains only if the street is wet".
  "Unless p, q" is !p -> q. "Q when p" is p -> q; where the text
  plainly means "only when", write q <-> p and say so in a comment.
- A sentence that only illustrates (for example ...), or says what the
  work will do (this chapter examines ...), states no claim: leave it
  out.

- Where the text raises an objection, write it as O1, O2, ... with a
  fourth field naming what it is against; a reply to an objection is
  R1, R2, ... against that objection:
  O1<TAB>S6<TAB>formula<TAB>against P1
  R1<TAB>S7<TAB>formula<TAB>against O1
  Write the objection as the text puts it, even if the text then
  answers it.
- Where a sentence gives a claim as someone else's (X argues, X holds,
  according to X, on X's view, a quotation with its author, a citation
  after the claim), write it as a premise with a fourth field naming
  whose it is, in one lower-case word: the author's surname, or the
  work's key if the text gives one:
  P3<TAB>S4<TAB>formula<TAB>says hobbes
  Write the claim as the text attributes it, whether or not the text
  agrees. Such a line is the text's report, not its own claim. Where
  the text also holds the claim itself, write it again as a plain
  premise at that sentence.
- Where it is plain what kind of claim a premise is, add a field after
  any other: kind empirical for how things are or were (what happened,
  what people or institutions did), kind normative for what ought to
  be, kind conceptual for what a word or concept means:
  P2<TAB>S2<TAB>formula<TAB>kind empirical
  Leave it out where it is not plain.
- Where the text speaks of what must or might be (necessarily,
  possibly), put a line "logic<TAB>s5" first and write [] for
  necessarily and <> for possibly: "it might rain" is <>rain. Where
  it speaks of what ought to be or is permitted (obligation,
  permission), put "logic<TAB>kd" first and write ought and
  permitted: "you ought to pay" is ought pay. Where it speaks of
  both, put "logic<TAB>s5kd" first and write [] and <> for what must
  or may be, ought and permitted for what must or may be done. Read
  each "must", "may", "can" or "should" in the sense its sentence
  gives it; where the sentence does not say which, write the sense
  you take and say so in a comment. "If p, then necessarily q" can
  say that the whole is necessary, [](p -> q), or that q is necessary
  once p holds, p -> []q: two different claims. Where a sentence
  could mean either, write the one you take and say in a comment that
  it could be read the other way. Such arguments take no all or some.
- Where the text speaks of all or some of a kind of thing, give the
  atom a parameter and quantify: "atom<TAB>audited(x)<TAB>x was
  audited", then "all x: audited(x) -> safe(x)" (every audited
  server is safe) or "some x: !patched(x)" (some server is
  unpatched). Name individuals in lower case: audited(web1). A line
  "domain<TAB>web1 web2" names individuals no statement mentions.
  An atom may relate two things, serves(x, y). The order of all and
  some is part of what is said: "every x serves some y" is all x:
  some y: serves(x, y), and "some y is served by every x" is some y:
  all x: serves(x, y). Write the order the sentence gives.

Translate what the text says, not what it should have said, and leave
nothing out that it claims.

Answer with the file and nothing else: no commentary, no code fences.
Lines beginning with # are comments and may say where you were
unsure.
